{"claim_id": "S21-20", "task_id": "m5-letter-sent", "claim": "M5 stands uncorrected by decision, not by oversight. The vdPS letter, already sent, says \"family (4.1) on p. 2655\"; the label (4.1) is displayed on p. 2654 and only the entries, including the b(z) at issue, are on p. 2655. The quoted entries are on the page the letter names, so the slip cannot mislead the recipients. Operator ruling: correct it in passing if a reply comes; do not send a correction for it alone. The notice itself has not been changed on this point.", "label": "INSPECTED", "evidence_type": "file_inspection", "dps": null, "reproducible": true, "script": "operator ruling; page image vdPS p. 2654", "output_hash": "f3ac6d41a1ed779bad0c600a93dcf23f683863cdaf41395f8262f1839c3bebde"}
{"claim_id": "S21-21", "task_id": "detector-defects", "claim": "Three defects in the CT-S21 consistency checker ITSELF, all found by reading its output rather than its exit code. (1) FALSE NEGATIVE FROM HARD WRAPPING: it reported the hedge \"No published erratum is known to us\" as missing. The sentence is present; it wraps across a blockquote line as \"No published\" / \"> erratum is known to us\". Trusting the checker would have prompted a repair to a sentence that was already correct. Phrase tests now run on text with blockquote markers stripped and whitespace collapsed. (2) A CHECK THAT COULD NO LONGER FAIL: the C4 test hardcoded the literal \"Four points\", so correcting the notice to \"Seven\" left it matching nothing and reporting 0 enumerated items with no verdict - passing silently. This is the seventh amendment's failure mode arising inside the gate built to catch it. It now parses the count word, maps it to a number, compares it against the enumerated items, and asserts the sentence exists at all. (3) A SUBSTRING SEARCH WITH NO POLARITY: the pattern author-confirmed matched the notice's own sentence \"nothing here is author-confirmed\", which asserts the opposite. Negated contexts are now separated and listed, never silently dropped.", "label": "INSPECTED", "evidence_type": "computation", "dps": null, "reproducible": true, "script": "scripts/item4_consistency.py", "output_hash": "39f6202418abe9cb944ed1e622e024c11cd4f64aabcbabc160f11542572566af"}
{"claim_id": "S21-22", "task_id": "detector-controls", "claim": "Controls for the patched detectors. scripts/control_item4.py mutates the notice, runs the REAL checker, restores the file and verifies the restore by sha256 (f3ac6d41a1ed779b...). All three fired: a count word disagreeing with the enumerated items produced MISMATCH; a genuine non-negated \"The authors have confirmed this\" produced an asserting F7 hit; a re-wrapped hedge was still found. Had any failed to fire, the corresponding detector would have been certifying nothing.", "label": "VERIFIED", "evidence_type": "computation", "dps": null, "reproducible": true, "script": "scripts/control_item4.py", "output_hash": "39f6202418abe9cb944ed1e622e024c11cd4f64aabcbabc160f11542572566af"}
{"claim_id": "S21-23", "task_id": "verdict-round2", "claim": "VERDICT REVISED: the v1.3 notice is READY to deposit, subject to two deposit-day confirmations - that no author reply has arrived, so C3's sentence still holds, and the standing citation checklist in the notice's final section. All six M-items are resolved: M1 by a C1 scope limitation plus the C5 second pass, with Remark 3.2 added and Remarks 4.2, 6.7 and Theorem 3.1 excluded with stated reasons; M2, M3 and M4 by edit and write-back; M5 left uncorrected by operator ruling; M6 by a default-deny exclusion verified by effect. Final state: support rows agree, cited ids exist and are unsuperseded, C4 says seven and enumerates seven, F7 has 0 asserting hits and 1 negated, and both hedges are present.", "label": "INSPECTED", "evidence_type": "file_inspection", "dps": null, "reproducible": true, "script": "C:\\LocalWork\\siarc-relay-bridge\\sessions\\2026-09-17\\CT-S21-DEPOSIT-PREP\\DEPOSIT-READY.md", "output_hash": "520684f62bf9fb6ee2ca175e8d2171c3dac631300ed89836562fbdb07a3e207b"}
{"claim_id": "S21-24", "task_id": "wongli-doi-confirmed", "claim": "Purchase target confirmed by DOI content negotiation on 2026-09-18, HTTP 200: DOI 10.1002/sapm1992874289 resolves to R. Wong and H. Li, \"Asymptotic Expansions for Second-Order Linear Difference Equations, II\", Studies in Applied Mathematics 87(4), 289-324, issued 1992-11. This matches the citation table in P0-CITATION-RULE.md exactly, including the numeral II and the word \"linear\" whose omission was the trap recorded at R1-61. The article itself remains NOT OBTAINED (R1-46 stands); nothing was purchased.", "label": "VERIFIED", "evidence_type": "computation", "dps": null, "reproducible": true, "script": "scripts/verify_refs.py", "output_hash": "897e92254b1050361c6b2d584c7823cb9c8ef8dea510e8e4963203672f76438c"}
{"claim_id": "S21-25", "task_id": "refs-acquired-ccby", "claim": "Two references acquired into CT-R1-AUDIT/refs/ after checking the licence printed on the source, and ONLY because it cleared: arXiv:2204.09305 = R. Wong, Yu-Qiu Zhao, \"Recent Advances in Asymptotic Analysis\", Anal. Appl. 20 (2022) 1103-1146, DOI 10.1142/S0219530522400012 (464879 bytes, sha256 123ab322619c723c...); and arXiv:2306.05930 = Alaa Ibrahim, Bruno Salvy, \"Positivity certificates for linear recurrences\", SODA 2024, DOI 10.1137/1.9781611977912.37 (720492 bytes, sha256 36e9d025c89b6fa0...). BOTH print http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/ on the arXiv abstract page, so both carry a redistribution right - unlike arXiv:0902.1702, whose default nonexclusive-distrib/1.0 grants none. These are the first third-party files in refs/ cleared for redistribution on evidence rather than left NOT CHECKED. The acquisition script refuses to download if the licence check does not pass.", "label": "VERIFIED", "evidence_type": "computation", "dps": null, "reproducible": true, "script": "scripts/acquire_refs.py", "output_hash": "cc290083afec658008199bb4bee3b8d5e8c76f3347d72501af49ca416be9272e"}
{"claim_id": "S21-26", "task_id": "salvy-content-unverified", "claim": "FLAG, NOT A FINDING. The operator describes arXiv:2306.05930 as stating that the leading coefficient of a linear recurrence dominant solution is not computable exactly in general. The IDENTIFIER is verified and the paper is real - Ibrahim and Salvy, \"Positivity certificates for linear recurrences\", SODA 2024 - but its title is about positivity certificates and does not obviously name that result, and the CONTENT CLAIM HAS NOT BEEN CHECKED against the PDF in this session. It is plausible that such a statement appears there, and equally possible the attribution belongs to a different source. It must be located and quoted before it is used to frame the contribution for K and r_infinity in v1.7. Recorded so the attribution does not travel unchecked; the same applies to the Wong-Zhao section 3 reading, which is the operator own and is not independently confirmed here.", "label": "CONJECTURED", "evidence_type": "file_inspection", "dps": null, "reproducible": true, "script": "scripts/verify_refs.py", "output_hash": "897e92254b1050361c6b2d584c7823cb9c8ef8dea510e8e4963203672f76438c"}
{"claim_id": "S21-27", "task_id": "wongli-letter-superseded", "claim": "AUTHOR-REQUEST-WONGLI.md marked SUPERSEDED - do not send, at its head. The correspondence route is dropped by operator ruling (R. Wong retired 2019, emeritus); the plan is purchase. The file is retained because the title trap it caught (R1-61) is still live: \"Wong & Li 1992a\" alone retrieves the wrong paper, and both \"linear\" and \"II\" are load-bearing. Correcting my own closing summary, which wrongly listed a \"Wong-Li letter\" and a pending Springer-contents check among the remaining queue: the letter is dropped, the contents check is done, and the letter that was actually sent was the vdPS typo report.", "label": "INSPECTED", "evidence_type": "file_inspection", "dps": null, "reproducible": true, "script": "C:\\LocalWork\\siarc-relay-bridge\\sessions\\2026-09-17\\CT-R1-AUDIT\\AUTHOR-REQUEST-WONGLI.md", "output_hash": "f76e761ce2c6f23011feacf47261a995f4bac3efbb967a406b323a8caa31560b"}
